Cover Crop Trial Summary:
- Crimson Clover alone: 68 kg/ha nitrogen retained | 54% weed suppression
- Winter Rye alone: 42 kg/ha nitrogen retained | 89% weed suppression
- Clover + Winter Rye (50/50 mix): 64 kg/ha nitrogen retained | 86% weed suppression
- Tillage Radish alone: 51 kg/ha nitrogen retained | 72% weed suppression

The researchers concluded that pairing a legume with a cereal grass allows farmers to capture nearly the full weed-suppression benefit of the grass without sacrificing the high nitrogen retention of the legume.', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the researchers'' conclusion?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "The Clover + Winter Rye mix achieved 86% weed suppression (close to Winter Rye''s 89%) while retaining 64 kg/ha of nitrogen (close to Crimson Clover''s 68 kg/ha)."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Tillage Radish retained 51 kg/ha of nitrogen and suppressed 72% of weeds, outperforming Winter Rye in nitrogen retention."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Crimson Clover retained 68 kg/ha of nitrogen, the highest amount of any treatment in the 120-day trial."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Winter Rye alone suppressed 89% of weeds, whereas Crimson Clover alone suppressed only 54% of weeds."}]'::jsonb, '{"type": "table", "caption": "120-Day Cover Crop Trial Results", "headers": ["Cover Crop Treatment", "Soil Nitrogen Retained (kg/ha)", "Weed Suppression (%)"], "rows": [["Crimson Clover (legume)", "68", "54%"], ["Winter Rye (cereal grass)", "42", "89%"], ["Clover + Winter Rye (50/50)", "64", "86%"], ["Tillage Radish (brassica)", "51", "72%"]]}'::jsonb, 'hdsat-open-core-2026', 'HDSAT Open Core Curated Bank', 'CC-BY-4.0', 'HDSAT Open Core Curated Bank (CC-BY-4.0)', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, status = EXCLUDED.status, updated_at = NOW();
Urban ecologist Marcus Vance contends that planting non-native drought-tolerant street trees in arid cities is an ecological mistake. Because native insect herbivores have not co-evolved with exotic foliage, non-native canopies support up to 70% fewer caterpillar species, depriving migratory songbirds of essential protein during spring stopovers.

Text 2
Landscape hydrologist Elena Rostova notes that in heavily paved metropolitan heat islands, soil compaction and radiant heat create microclimates several degrees hotter and drier than the surrounding native desert. In her multi-year survey of Phoenix boulevards, several native riparian tree species suffered high canopy dieback, whereas selected non-native Mediterranean oaks maintained dense shade that cooled adjacent sidewalks by 6°C and allowed understory native shrubs to survive.', 'Based on the texts, how would Rostova (Text 2) most likely respond to Vance''s argument (Text 1)?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "By disputing Vance''s finding that migratory songbirds rely on caterpillars during spring stopovers."}, {"id": "B", "text": "By arguing that urban planners should replace all street trees with artificial shade structures to conserve water."}, {"id": "C", "text": "By agreeing that non-native Mediterranean oaks should be removed from arid cities immediately."}, {"id": "D", "text": "By pointing out that in extreme urban microclimates where native trees struggle to survive, resilient non-native trees can provide microclimate cooling that indirectly supports native understory plants."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hdsat-benchmark-sim-1', 'HDSAT Benchmark Simulation Set A (Released Practice Form)', 'CC-BY-4.0', 'HDSAT Benchmark Simulation Set A (Released Practice Form) (CC-BY-4.0)', TRUE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, status = EXCLUDED.status, updated_at = NOW();
- Sodium-ion batteries use abundant sodium salts rather than lithium and cobalt.
- Current commercial sodium-ion cells have a gravimetric energy density of 140–160 Wh/kg, compared with 240–280 Wh/kg for nickel-manganese-cobalt (NMC) lithium-ion cells.
- Sodium-ion cells retain over 88% of their discharge capacity at -20°C, whereas standard lithium-ion cells often drop below 65% capacity at that temperature.
- Because stationary grid storage facilities do not move, battery weight is far less important than cold-weather reliability and raw-material cost.', 'The student wants to explain why sodium-ion batteries are particularly well suited for stationary cold-climate grid storage despite having lower energy density than NMC lithium-ion cells. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "At -20°C, standard lithium-ion cells often drop below 65% discharge capacity, while sodium-ion batteries have an energy density of 140–160 Wh/kg."}, {"id": "B", "text": "NMC lithium-ion cells achieve an energy density of 240–280 Wh/kg, whereas sodium-ion cells use abundant sodium salts instead of lithium and cobalt."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Although sodium-ion batteries have a lower energy density (140–160 Wh/kg) than NMC lithium-ion cells, their heavier weight matters little in stationary grid facilities, where their low-cost sodium materials and >88% capacity retention at -20°C are major advantages."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Stationary grid storage facilities do not move, so engineers must choose between NMC lithium-ion batteries and sodium-ion batteries."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hdsat-open-core-2026', 'HDSAT Open Core Curated Bank', 'CC-BY-4.0', 'HDSAT Open Core Curated Bank (CC-BY-4.0)', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, status = EXCLUDED.status, updated_at = NOW();
- In 1977, the Voyager 1 and 2 spacecraft launched carrying the Golden Record, a 12-inch gold-plated copper phonograph disc.
- The committee led by Carl Sagan selected 115 analog-encoded images and 90 minutes of audio.
- Acoustic engineer Timothy Ferris oversaw the mastering of the record at 16 2/3 revolutions per minute (rpm) rather than the standard 33 1/3 rpm LP speed.
- Halving the rotational speed doubled the playing time that could fit into the disc''s physical grooves, though it slightly reduced high-frequency audio fidelity.', 'The student wants to emphasize the engineering trade-off involved in mastering the Voyager Golden Record. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "Timothy Ferris oversaw the mastering of the Golden Record, which spun at 16 2/3 revolutions per minute rather than the standard 33 1/3 rpm."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Carl Sagan''s committee included both analog-encoded images and audio recordings on the 12-inch copper phonograph disc."}, {"id": "C", "text": "By mastering the Voyager Golden Record at 16 2/3 rpm instead of 33 1/3 rpm, Timothy Ferris doubled the audio duration that could fit on the disc at the cost of a slight reduction in high-frequency fidelity."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Launched in 1977 aboard Voyager 1 and 2, the 12-inch gold-plated copper Golden Record contained 115 images and 90 minutes of audio selected by Carl Sagan''s committee."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hdsat-benchmark-sim-1', 'HDSAT Benchmark Simulation Set A (Released Practice Form)', 'CC-BY-4.0', 'HDSAT Benchmark Simulation Set A (Released Practice Form) (CC-BY-4.0)', TRUE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, status = EXCLUDED.status, updated_at = NOW();
